// The theme: a value handed to a whole subtree, and the attribute the stylesheets select on.
//
// **THE THEME IS IN THE URL, AND THAT IS THE DECISION THIS FILE IS MADE OF.** `?theme=dark` is
// something a person can bookmark, send to somebody else and press back out of; the same choice put
// in `useState` vanishes on reload, and put in `localStorage` is invisible to the server -- which
// means the server renders a light page, the browser adopts it, and the page then flips to dark in
// front of the reader. A server that can read the choice off the request renders the right page the
// first time, and there is nothing to flash.
//
// **The cookie form is the same component with the value handed in**, and it is what an application
// picks when it would rather not spend a query parameter. See `Theme`'s note below.

import { createElement, Fragment, createContext, useContext, style } from lath
import { useSearch } from lath/router

import tokens from "./tokens.css"
import sheet from "./theme.css"
import { choice, classes, maybeFn, list } from "./props.sl"

// The two words, in one place, so that a message listing them cannot drift from the check.
export val Themes = ["light", "dark"]

// What a `useTheme` with no `Theme` above it is holding.
//
// **A default rather than a fault**, which is `useContext`'s own rule and is what lets a single
// component be rendered on its own in a test. The setter is the half that cannot have a sensible
// default, so it says so.
noTheme(next) =
    throw "useTheme's setter needs a `Theme` above the component that called it, and there is none"

val Chosen = createContext({ theme: "light", set: noTheme })

// The theme, and a way to change it.
//
//     val [theme, setTheme] = useTheme()
//
// **It is not a hook** -- `useContext` keeps no slot -- so unlike `useState` it may be called inside
// a condition, which is the ordinary case for a component that only sometimes renders the thing the
// theme is about.
export useTheme() -> array =
    val held = useContext(Chosen)

    [held.theme, held.set]

// The provider, and the element every stylesheet in this package selects a dark page on.
//
// **Two arrangements, and the prop is which one.**
//
// | | |
// |---|---|
// | `<Theme>` with no `theme` | the URL: `?theme=dark`, read with `useSearch` and rewritten by `set` |
// | `<Theme theme={…} onChange={…}>` | the caller's: a cookie the server read, a column on an account |
//
// **The URL form is the one to reach for**, and the reason is the server: a page rendered for
// `/?theme=dark` is dark in the markup, so there is no first paint in the wrong colours and nothing
// for a hydrating page to correct. The cookie form buys a URL with nothing in it and costs the
// application a `Set-Cookie` route and a `back` field; it is written here because an application
// that already has an accounts table would rather keep the choice there.
//
// **`set` REWRITES the address rather than pushing it**, which is `useSearch`'s own default and the
// right one here: changing a theme is not somewhere a person went, and a push would make the back
// button undo the colour rather than the page.
//
// **`theme=light` is the absence of the parameter.** Writing the default out would put a parameter
// on every URL anybody copied, so `set("light")` takes it off.
//
// **`set` KEEPS THE REST OF THE QUERY**, which is not a nicety: a theme toggle on
// `/?tag=slate&sort=busiest` that wrote only its own name would throw the filter and the sort order
// away, and the page a person was reading would be gone along with the colour they wanted changed.
//
// **A `?theme` nobody recognises is the default, and says nothing.** The address is whatever a
// person typed or a link they followed carried, so `/?theme=chartreuse` is not a mistake anybody
// reading the page can fix -- and a fault there is a 500 on a server for a value a stranger chose.
// The `theme` *prop* is the opposite case: it comes from the program, so a word that is neither of
// the two still refuses and still names the prop.
export theme({ theme = null, onChange = null, class: extra = "", children = [] }) =
    style(tokens)
    style(sheet)

    val given = if theme == null then null else choice("Theme", "theme", theme, Themes)
    val handler = maybeFn("Theme", "onChange", onChange)
    val kids = list("Theme", "children", children)

    // **The hook is taken whether or not this render uses it**, which is the rule every hook has: a
    // `Theme` whose `theme` prop arrives on the second render would otherwise change its own hook
    // layout between renders.
    val search = useSearch()
    val query = search[0]
    val write = search[1]

    // What the address asked for, and the default where it asked for something that is not a theme.
    val asked = query.theme ?? "light"
    val inUrl = if asked is string && Themes.indexOf(asked) != null then asked else "light"

    val now = if given != null then given else inUrl

    // Where a change goes, which is the whole difference between the two arrangements.
    set(next) =
        val wanted = choice("Theme", "theme", next, Themes)

        if given != null
            if handler == null
                throw "Theme was given a `theme`, so changing it needs an `onChange`, and there is none"

            handler(wanted)
        else
            write(query with { theme: if wanted == "light" then null else wanted })

        null

    val out = <Chosen.Provider value={{ theme: now, set: set }}>
        <div class={classes("Theme", "mortar", extra)} data-theme={now}>{kids}</div>
    </Chosen.Provider>

    out
